![Photo by Ilya Pavlov on Unsplash](cover.jpg)

A personal website is a great ally for documenting our path and
evolution in the IT industry.

## Github Is Insufficient

Many may think that just uploading our code to platforms like
_Github_, _Gitlab_, _Codeberg_ and similar is enough. But
unfortunately they have the following problems:

- They don't work for documenting private projects.
- The information isn't organized and it's hard to highlight the
  important parts.
- You depend on a third party and have no autonomy over storing and
  distributing your content.

The main problem is when you participate in projects where you
don't own the code — normally for private companies. On your
website you can at least document that you participated in those
projects (if your contract allows it).

Also, often there are so many scattered projects that it's better to
have a centralized place you can put on your resume.

## Start Today

Ideally, if you're a student or learning, start right now to have a
personal site that documents the projects, ideas, events and other
experiences you've lived in your professional career. If you already
have some years behind you, it will be a bit harder to start
documenting everything and it will take more time to organize the
material you've accumulated over the years.

## What technology to use?

The most important thing is having migration capability. If some
event causes your hosting provider to shut down, you can easily
find another and migrate your content. Plus being able to be
stored on [archive.org](https://web.archive.org) for historical
preservation.

The recommendation is to use static websites with the trusty old
HTML and CSS technologies.

### Use a static site generator

Ideally use one built with the technology you like most; there are
diverse options for all tastes.

Here's a small selection

```github
geolessel/vox | Un generador de sitios estáticos escrito en Elixir
```

```github
gohugoio/hugo | El framework más rápido del mundo para construir sitios web
```

```github
11ty/eleventy | Un generador de sitios estáticos más simple
```

### Build your own content generator

It's a good way to practice a language or technology — a small
project that adapts to your needs. Though this option is only for
those who want to spend a lot of time on details that available
generators have already solved.

## Buy Your Domain and Hosting

Ideally you should have your own domain and hosting. You can start
with Github Pages hosting to get going.

### Domain Providers

- <https://nic.cl> : Chilean domains
- <https://dyndns.com> : Domains for IPs
- <https://www.dynadot.com> : International domains
- <https://porkbun.com/> : Another domain provider.

### Hosting Providers

Depending on your needs you can have something on a Raspberry PI,
or get hosting. The following is an unordered list of different
options.

- <https://cpanelhost.cl/> : Cheap Chilean Hosting
- <https://www.silverhost.cl/> : Medium Chilean Hosting
- <https://pages.cloudflare.com/> : Static Page Hosting
- <https://pages.github.com/> : Static Page Hosting
- <https://codeberg.page/> : Static Page Hosting
- <https://neocities.org/> : Static Page Hosting
- Own Server (Raspberry PI)
- VPS Servers (DigitalOcean, Vultr, etc)
- Application Servers (Fly.io, Vercel)
- Cloud Servers (AWS, Azure, GCP)
